# Introduction # {#introduction}

This paper proposes homogeneous function parameter packs for C++29, allowing a single declaration to replace a family of fixed-arity overloads while retaining ordinary conversions. Such a pack would have one declared element type and use ordinary parameter initialization.

Note: C++26 clause references use N5050. [[N5050]]

<figure class=table id=pack-matrix>
<figcaption id=pack-matrix-caption>Independent parameter-pack declarations. C++26 permits all but the proposed lower-right form.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison" aria-labelledby=pack-matrix-caption>
<colgroup><col><col><col></colgroup>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Element types</th><th scope=col>Template parameter pack</th><th scope=col>Function parameter pack</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope=row>Heterogeneous</th>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
template <auto... vs>
void f();
</xmp>
</td>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
void f(auto... vs);
</xmp>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope=row>Homogeneous</th>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Legal since C++11: T is not a parameter pack, but vs is.
template <class T, T... vs>
void foo();
</xmp>
</td>
<td class=code-invalid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Proposed (ill-formed in C++26): vs is a pack of T parameters.
template <class T>
void foo(T... vs);
</xmp>
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

As Table 1 shows, the symmetry between template and function parameter packs is incomplete: C++26 lacks the homogeneous function form. In the lower-right declaration, <code>T</code> names one type, leaving <code>T... vs</code> with no pack to expand. The declaration is therefore ill-formed in current C++. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.spec.auto.general#2">[dcl.spec.auto.general]/2</a> <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=250">[dcl.fct]/24</a> Under our proposal, <code>vs</code> would instead be a pack of parameters, each of type <code>T</code>.

When an element type <code>T</code> is fixed or explicitly supplied, ordinary call-site conversions can initialize the parameters from arguments of different source types. In contrast, if <code>T</code> is deduced instead, the applicable arguments must yield consistent deductions. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type#2">[temp.deduct.type]/2</a> In either case, the pack's length is known at compile time even when the argument values are computed at runtime. Those values initialize function parameters, whereas a homogeneous template pack takes constant template arguments. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#9">[temp.variadic]/9</a>

For example, Abseil's <code>Partial</code> needs the number of arguments to determine its result type while their values may be computed at runtime. Its authors also want conversions at the call so diagnostics can distinguish a constant argument from a signed variable. Current C++ can combine these properties through generated fixed-arity overloads, but a homogeneous pack would express them in one declaration.

CasADi's matrix concatenation also relies on fixed parameter types for overload resolution. In constructed calls, they select an overload where constrained deduced packs leave the call ambiguous. A homogeneous pack would preserve that selection while replacing the positional overload family with one declaration per operation. We examine both library comparisons in <a href="#examples">Section 3</a>.

Continued requests for fixed-type variadic interfaces provide further motivation ([[#expressivity]]).

The proposal allows one trailing homogeneous pack, making an abbreviated function template whose specializations have ordinary fixed-arity function types. Existing pack expansions retain their meanings.

Earlier revisions received EWGI support, though the EWG poll to accept P1219 for C++23 failed in Belfast in 2019. [[P1219-EWG-RECORD]] Since then, C++26 has deprecated the comma-less C-varargs spelling that conflicts with unnamed homogeneous packs. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a> We return with concrete library comparisons, further evidence of programmer demand, and a revised model for the pack count.

Our paper though does not consider declaration-site length syntax or multiple homogeneous packs. A single trailing pack covers the library use cases below and lets the remaining call arguments determine its length. Declaration-site lengths would require new syntax and multiple packs would need rules for dividing arguments between them ([[#explicit-lengths]]).

Within this scope, the unambiguous named form <code>int... values</code> can proceed independently of the <a href="#compatibility">removal and reclamation decision</a>. Unnamed packs, however, require reclaiming <code>int...</code>, which still denotes C varargs in C++26. Deprecation alone does not establish that reclamation is safe. We therefore retain both decisions in P1219R3 with <a href="#proposed-polls">separate proposed EWG polls</a>. If EWG feedback warrants it, a separate removal paper remains a fallback.

# Library use cases # {#examples}

The following examples examine how homogeneous packs could simplify interfaces in Abseil and CasADi. In each case, we compare the proposed declaration with the shipped implementation and alternatives available in current C++.

The current and proposed definitions in Tables 2 and 3 are independent alternatives.

## Combine call-site diagnostics with count-dependent results ## {#example-count}

Abseil's internal <code>Layout&lt;Ts...&gt;</code> describes one array per element type in a single allocation. When the first arrays' element counts are supplied, <code>Partial</code> records the number of arguments in its result type <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code>, while the size values themselves may be computed at runtime. [[ABSL-LAYOUT]]

For example, with runtime <code>size_t</code> values <code>int_count</code> and <code>double_count</code>:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
using ArrayLayout =
    absl::container_internal::Layout<int, double, char>;

auto ints_known = ArrayLayout::Partial(int_count);
auto ints_and_doubles_known =
    ArrayLayout::Partial(int_count, double_count);
</xmp>

These calls return <code>ArrayLayout::PartialType&lt;1&gt;</code> and <code>ArrayLayout::PartialType&lt;2&gt;</code>, respectively. The first permits querying <code>int</code> and <code>double</code> offsets, while the second also permits querying the <code>char</code> offset:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto double_offset = ints_known.Offset<double>();
auto char_offset = ints_and_doubles_known.Offset<char>();
</xmp>

In contrast, <code>ints_known.Offset&lt;char&gt;()</code> is rejected at compile time because the preceding <code>double</code> size is missing. A replacement must therefore retain both the count-selected result types and their available offsets, even though the offset values depend on runtime sizes. [[ABSL-LAYOUT]]

To obtain that count, the shipped declaration deduces every argument type. In the body, it casts each argument to <code>size_t</code>. A homogeneous pack would instead deduce only the count and initialize <code>size_t</code> parameters at the call:

<figure class=table id=partial-comparison>
<figcaption id=partial-comparison-caption>Abseil's <code>Partial</code> and a proposed homogeneous declaration. Both retain the argument-count check.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison" aria-labelledby=partial-comparison-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Abseil at d522178</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Sizes>
static constexpr PartialType<sizeof...(Sizes)>
Partial(Sizes&&... sizes) {
  static_assert(sizeof...(Sizes) +
      StaticSizeSeq::size() <= sizeof...(Ts));
  return PartialType<sizeof...(Sizes)>(
      static_cast<size_t>(
          std::forward<Sizes>(sizes))...);
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
static constexpr auto
Partial(size_t... sizes)
    -> PartialType<sizeof...(sizes)> {
  static_assert(sizeof...(sizes) +
      StaticSizeSeq::size() <= sizeof...(Ts));
  return PartialType<sizeof...(sizes)>(
      sizes...);
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

For <code>int signed_count</code>, Abseil's authors want warnings for <code>ArrayLayout::Partial(signed_count)</code> but not <code>ArrayLayout::Partial(3)</code>. Their <a href="https://github.com/abseil/abseil-cpp/blob/d522178b54087d2c3855484c1899ce99ad278169/absl/container/internal/layout.h#L760-L780">comment</a> explains that explicit casts suppress both warnings, while implicit forwarding-body conversions can warn for both.

Fixed <code>size_t</code> parameters make this distinction available by exposing the original argument expression to conversion diagnostics at the call. A <code>std::convertible_to&lt;size_t&gt;</code> constraint, however, leaves conversions in the body. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/concept.convertible#1">[concept.convertible]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#6">[expr.call]/6</a>

The warnings still depend on the implementation and options. Negative integers remain well-formed and convert to unsigned values. Also, ordinary parameter initialization rejects explicit-only conversions, such as an explicit <code>operator size_t()</code>, that the shipped <code>static_cast</code> accepts. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/conv.integral#3">[conv.integral]/3</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.conv#1">[over.match.conv]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.static.cast#5">[expr.static.cast]/5</a>

Current C++ can also supply the same conversions and result types through a bounded overload set. For plain <code>Layout&lt;Ts...&gt;</code> without static sizes, we can generate arities zero through <code>sizeof...(Ts)</code>. The header already uses the underlying technique, expanding an index pack through <code>IntToSize</code> to give its <a href="https://github.com/abseil/abseil-cpp/blob/d522178b54087d2c3855484c1899ce99ad278169/absl/container/internal/layout.h#L399-L400">constructor</a> <code>size_t</code> parameters. In the following example, <code>LayoutType&lt;N, Ts...&gt;</code> stands for <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code>:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <size_t>
using IntToSize = size_t;

template <class Seq, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverload;

template <size_t... Is, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverload<std::index_sequence<Is...>, Ts...> {
  static constexpr LayoutType<sizeof...(Is), Ts...>
  Partial(IntToSize<Is>... sizes) {
    return LayoutType<sizeof...(Is), Ts...>(sizes...);
  }
};

template <class Seq, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverloads;

template <size_t... Ns, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverloads<std::index_sequence<Ns...>, Ts...>
    : PartialOverload<std::make_index_sequence<Ns>, Ts...>... {
  using PartialOverload<std::make_index_sequence<Ns>, Ts...>::Partial...;
};
</xmp>

To expose the overload set, <code>Layout</code> inherits <code>PartialOverloads&lt;std::make_index_sequence&lt;sizeof...(Ts) + 1&gt;, Ts...&gt;</code>.

A homogeneous pack replaces this generation and combination with one declaration.

## Preserve conversion ranking without fixed-arity wrappers ## {#example-casadi}

CasADi builds symbolic matrix expressions, with <code>horzcat</code>, <code>vertcat</code>, and <code>diagcat</code> providing horizontal, vertical, and diagonal concatenation. At d30fbc4, these operations have fifteen positional wrappers covering arities two through six. Their <code>const MatType&amp;</code> parameter types are fixed by the enclosing <code>SparsityInterface</code> specialization. [[CASADI-CONCAT]] For example, the library call <a href="https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/d30fbc46a6cc7ca18b6e63f63d586069beaacd11/casadi/core/integration_tools.cpp#L930-L936"><code>vertcat(dx_cat, 1)</code></a> relies on scalar conversion to that matrix type.

<figure class=table id=casadi-comparison>
<figcaption id=casadi-comparison-caption>CasADi's vertical concatenation wrappers, with two arities shown, and a proposed replacement for the family. Both initialize references to a fixed matrix type.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison" aria-labelledby=casadi-comparison-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>CasADi at d30fbc4</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
inline friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& x, const MatType& y) {
  return vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{x, y});
}

inline friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& x, const MatType& y,
    const MatType& z) {
  return vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{x, y, z});
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& first,
    const MatType& second,
    const MatType&... rest) {
  return MatType::vertcat(
      std::vector<MatType>{first, second, rest...});
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

In the proposed declaration, the fixed parameters retain the two-operand minimum, while the trailing pack removes the six-argument ceiling. The same change would apply to horizontal and diagonal concatenation.

A constrained deduced pack in the same class also replaces the overload family:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Args>
  requires (sizeof...(Args) >= 2 &&
            (std::is_convertible_v<
                const Args&, const MatType&> && ...))
friend MatType vertcat(const Args&... args) {
  return MatType::vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{args...});
}
</xmp>

CasADi's <code>SX</code> and <code>IM</code> are symbolic-expression and integer matrices. For mutable <code>SX symbolic</code> and <code>IM indices</code>, <a href="https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/d30fbc46a6cc7ca18b6e63f63d586069beaacd11/casadi/core/generic_matrix.hpp#L285-L288">indexing</a> yields a <code>SubIndex&lt;SX, IM&gt;</code> proxy derived from <code>SX</code>. [[CASADI-PROXY]] Now consider these constructed calls and how overload resolution changes:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
vertcat(symbolic(indices), 1);
vertcat(1, symbolic(indices));
</xmp>

Argument-dependent lookup finds concatenation functions for <code>SX</code> and the proxy's <code>IM</code> template argument. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/basic.lookup.argdep#3">[basic.lookup.argdep]/3</a> Fixed parameters select <code>SX</code> in both calls because binding the proxy to its base outranks conversion to <code>IM</code>. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.ics.rank#2.1">[over.ics.rank]/2.1</a> If we consider a deduced pack instead, both candidates bind the deduced types directly and satisfy the constraints. Neither wins. Therefore, both calls are ambiguous. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.best.general#2">[over.match.best.general]/2</a> The homogeneous declaration retains the fixed destinations for conversion ranking.

Fixing only the first parameter as <code>const MatType&amp;</code> still leaves the second call ambiguous. Even two fixed parameters leave <code>vertcat(1, 1, symbolic(indices))</code> ambiguous because the distinguishing operand is in the deduced tail.

Competing <code>std::initializer_list&lt;SX&gt;</code> and <code>std::initializer_list&lt;IM&gt;</code> overloads also make grouped calls ambiguous, though for a different reason. List ranking uses the worst element conversion. Here the scalar needs a user-defined conversion to either destination. This erases the indexed operand's distinction. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.ics.list#5">[over.ics.list]/5</a>

Current C++ does permit arbitrary counts when the destination is explicit, such as in <code>SX::vertcat({symbolic(indices), 1})</code>. Existing or generated fixed-arity wrappers also preserve positional calls. A homogeneous pack would express those wrappers' conversions with one declaration per operation and no fixed upper bound.

# Motivation and existing alternatives # {#motivation}

The existing solutions differ in how they provide call-site diagnostics, count-dependent results, and conversion ranking.

## Demand for fixed-type variadic interfaces ## {#expressivity}

<p>A variadic interface need not deduce a separate type for every argument. When the author has already chosen the parameter type, the remaining variation is the number of parameters. Homogeneous packs would express that distinction directly. The examples in R2 and later requests for fixed-type positional interfaces show that this requirement recurs. [[P1219R2]] [[REDDIT-ENUM-PACK]] [[SO-STRING-VIEW-CONCAT]]</p>

<p>Declaring the type also determines how each parameter is initialized. With deduced argument types, supplying an intended type separately can leave conversions in the function body. [[SO-TYPED-ACCUMULATOR]] This distinction matters for ordinary conversion ranking [[LOEW-HOMOGENEOUS-OVERLOADS]] and for by-value parameter initialization without additional construction in the body. [[MAZIERES-PARAM-PACK]] A homogeneous pack with a fixed element type would use ordinary parameter initialization at the call. Its count would also remain available for constraints, such as requiring an even number of arguments. [[SO-10]]</p>

<p>These discussions provide solutions in current C++, including constrained packs and emulations with fixed parameter types. The library comparisons above show why the choice matters: constraints on deduced types do not supply the conversion ranking of fixed parameters, while generated fixed-arity overloads require additional machinery. Homogeneous packs would express those fixed parameter types in one variadic declaration while retaining a compile-time count.</p>

## Initializer lists and vectors ## {#initializer-lists}

<figure class=table id=homogeneous-interfaces>
<figcaption id=homogeneous-interfaces-caption>Homogeneous interfaces, each permitting a fixed element type. The vector column excludes <code>bool</code>.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=homogeneous-interfaces-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Property</th><th scope=col>Homogeneous pack</th><th scope=col><code>std::initializer_list&lt;T&gt;</code></th><th scope=col><code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;</code></th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope=row>Elements and mutation</th><td>Value or reference parameters with declared cv-qualification</td><td>Const backing-array elements</td><td>Owned elements, mutable through a non-const vector</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Ownership and lifetime</th><td>Values owned, references borrowed under ordinary lifetime rules</td><td>View. Copies neither copy elements nor extend backing-array lifetime</td><td>Owns storage and elements. Reference parameters borrow</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Contiguous storage</th><td>No array or contiguity guarantee</td><td>Contiguous backing array</td><td>Contiguous storage</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Count and iteration</th><td>Template count. Pack expansion, folds, or pack indexing</td><td><code>size()</code> and ordinary iteration</td><td><code>size()</code> and ordinary iteration</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Runtime-sized input</th><td>No direct runtime-range expansion into arguments</td><td>No arbitrary runtime-range constructor</td><td>Accepts runtime-sized input and can grow</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Storage and element work</th><td>Ordinary parameter initialization, no container allocation required</td><td>Backing-array initialization</td><td>Storage construction can allocate and construct elements. Passing an existing vector by reference does neither</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Call syntax</th><td><code>f(a, b, c)</code></td><td><code>f({a, b, c})</code></td><td><code>f(existing)</code> or vector construction, such as <code>f({a, b, c})</code></td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Function declarations</th><td>Template with one trailing homogeneous pack</td><td>Can be non-template and precede other parameters</td><td>Can be non-template and precede other parameters</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>


Although container sizes can be constant-evaluable, an ordinary function parameter's value still cannot supply a count-dependent return type or select a specialization. The proposed template count would instead be independent of runtime values, as Abseil's <code>PartialType</code> requires ([[#example-count]]). <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/support.initlist">[support.initlist]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#10">[temp.variadic]/10</a>

There is also a difference in conversion ranking: initializer lists lose CasADi's per-parameter matrix distinction ([[#example-casadi]]). Constructor overload selection introduces a further difference, which we discuss in [[#constructors]].

For existing vector inputs, runtime-only lengths, ownership, iteration, or growth, vectors remain preferable. Passing an existing vector as <code>const std::vector&lt;T&gt;&amp;</code> avoids copying and <code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;&amp;</code> also permits mutation. By-value passing copies supported lvalues or can transfer rvalue storage through the ordinary move constructor. These calls need no temporary vector. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/vector.overview">[vector.overview]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/container.reqmts">[container.reqmts]</a>

The way a vector is constructed matters for move-only elements. Its initializer-list constructor uses const T references, generally preventing <code>std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> construction from braced owning pointers. Suitable insertion or range construction still supports move-only elements. Homogeneous value parameters could instead move-initialize those elements and references could borrow them. Initializer lists themselves can contain move-only objects, but const access prevents ordinary moves out. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/sequence.reqmts">[sequence.reqmts]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/initializer.list.syn">[initializer.list.syn]</a>

Wrappers provide some flexibility within these restrictions. Lists of <code>std::reference_wrapper&lt;T&gt;</code> can mutate caller-owned objects. User-defined wrappers with <code>mutable</code> members also permit ownership transfer, though list copies share those members and observe moved-from state after consumption through another copy. Neither technique changes backing-array constness or makes the count a template argument. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.stc#9">[dcl.stc]/9</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list#5">[dcl.init.list]/5</a>

These interface differences do not give homogeneous packs a general performance advantage. Parameter initialization can copy or move, bodies can allocate, and vectors can reuse storage. Also, count specializations can increase instantiation work and code size despite possible code sharing. These costs therefore need to be measured for the intended use.

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: research. Jan: review.</strong> Compare typed runtime variadics and checked C varargs, including D's variadics and GCC's argument-count builtin. Separate count and type checks, accounting for default promotions. <a href="https://dlang.org/spec/function.html#typesafe_variadic_functions">D typesafe variadics</a> <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Constructing-Calls.html#index-_005f_005fbuiltin_005fva_005farg_005fpack_005flen">GCC <code>__builtin_va_arg_pack_len</code></a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#12">[expr.call]/12</a></p>
</aside>

## Spans, arrays, and constrained ranges ## {#range-alternatives}

When the caller already has contiguous storage, spans and array references are suitable alternatives. Homogeneous packs would retain individual initialization and overload ranking without requiring a common buffer.

For example, <code>std::span&lt;T&gt;</code> borrows contiguous, runtime-sized input for ordinary iteration without allocation, copying, ownership, or lifetime extension. Whether its elements can be mutated depends on <code>T</code>, not the span's constness: <code>std::span&lt;const T&gt;</code> provides read-only access. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.overview">[span.overview]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.elem">[span.elem]</a>

Unlike dynamic spans, <code>std::span&lt;T, N&gt;</code> encodes its length so that a deduced <code>N</code> can be used in a return type or constraint. Runtime-sized input must still have length <code>N</code> and cannot deduce it. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.cons#18">[span.cons]/18</a> <code>std::array&lt;T, N&gt;</code> and references to <code>T&#91;N]</code> also supply compile-time counts, supporting Abseil's <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code> today. For example, the following calls deduce the extent: <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.overview">[span.overview]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/array#overview">[array.overview]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t N>
auto span_count(std::span<const std::size_t, N>) {
    return std::integral_constant<std::size_t, N>{};
}

template <std::size_t N>
auto array_count(const std::size_t (&)[N]) {
    return std::integral_constant<std::size_t, N>{};
}

const std::size_t sizes[]{3, 5};
auto from_span = span_count(std::span{sizes});
auto from_array = array_count(sizes);
auto from_braces = array_count({3, 5});
</xmp>

All three results are <code>std::integral_constant&lt;std::size_t, 2&gt;</code>. In particular, <code>array_count({3, 5})</code> deduces the bound and initializes a temporary array. An analogous array-reference <code>Partial({int_count, double_count})</code> could therefore return <code>PartialType&lt;2&gt;</code> without named storage or explicit extent. This would change Abseil's signed-variable case, though, because list-initialization rejects narrowing permitted by ordinary <code>size_t</code> parameters ([[#example-count]]). When binding an existing array, no elements are copied, mutation is possible through mutable references, and lifetime is not extended. Zero extent is another distinction: static spans and <code>std::array</code> permit it, whereas built-in arrays require positive bounds. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/class.temporary#6.10">[class.temporary]/6.10</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.array#1">[dcl.array]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/array.zero">[array.zero]</a>

Conversions also depend on how the input is supplied. Span construction from existing arrays or ranges permits qualification conversions but no numeric or user-defined element conversions. For example, an <code>int</code> array cannot initialize <code>std::span&lt;const std::size_t&gt;</code> despite individual integer conversion to <code>size_t</code>. Constructing a typed array or vector chooses the destination first. A fixed const-element span also accepts double braces such as <code>&#123;&#123;1, 2}}</code>. This initializes a temporary array with numeric or user-defined conversions while rejecting narrowing. The array survives through the call's full-expression. These grouped forms still retain neither positional syntax nor CasADi's per-parameter ranking ([[#example-casadi]]). <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.cons#13">[span.cons]/13</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.cons#16">[span.cons]/16</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/class.temporary#6.10">[class.temporary]/6.10</a>

With <code>std::array&lt;T, N&gt;</code>, the object owns fixed contiguous storage and permits mutation when non-const. Passing it by reference avoids element copies. Passing it by value can copy or move, though direct prvalue initialization need not. An explicit <code>T</code> supplies list-element destinations with narrowing checks, whereas class template argument deduction requires identical element types. This therefore retains the count and fixed type but requires a grouped object. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/array#overview">[array.overview]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/array.cons">[array.cons]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init#general">[dcl.init.general]</a>

For iteration over runtime-sized or non-contiguous sequences, a constrained <code>std::ranges::input_range</code> is another alternative. The concept guarantees neither contiguity, a size operation, mutation, ownership, nor static extent. Passing a forwarding reference can borrow without element copies. Also, deduction of the concrete type can preserve a type-carried static extent, as in static spans and arrays. Element convertibility requirements can constrain conversions, but conversions during iteration remain in the body rather than at the original positional call ([[#example-count]]). <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/range.refinements">[range.refinements]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/range.sized">[range.sized]</a>

# Design decisions # {#design-decisions}

## Definitions ## {#definitions}

For each <em>homogeneous function parameter pack</em> (<em>homogeneous pack</em>), the proposal invents a constant template parameter for its length.

We classify function parameter packs by their <em>parameter pattern</em>, which is the declaration without the declarator ellipsis. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#6.1">[temp.variadic]/6.1</a> Ordinary placeholder processing first invents a type parameter pack for <code>auto...</code>. <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=249">[dcl.fct]/21</a> We then classify the declaration before substituting enclosing template arguments: it is homogeneous only if its pattern contains no unexpanded pack. An ordinary expansion therefore remains an expansion even when its resulting parameter types are identical.

Each row in Table 5 is independent. <code>T</code> denotes one type, <code>Ts</code> a type pack, <code>Templates</code> a compatible template template parameter pack, <code>Is</code> an index pack, and <code>I</code> a constant index. <code>Box</code> takes a type pack and <code>C</code> is a unary type concept.

The rule covers <code>xs</code> as a constant template parameter, function parameter, init-capture, or structured binding pack.

Each homogeneous case gets its own count, but a zero count does not avoid substitution into the element type. Types, indices, folds, and array bounds must still be valid. Also, the last row's nested requirement determines the value of a <code>bool</code> requires-expression without constraining the function.

For this classification, an occurrence is unexpanded unless it is inside a nested expansion's pattern. Indexing consumes the indexed pack name, but leaves unexpanded packs in the index for an enclosing expansion. Neither <code>decltype</code> nor ordinary <code>sizeof</code> consumes packs. These cases follow the existing expansion boundaries <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#6">[temp.variadic]/6</a> and constraint rules <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.fold">[temp.constr.fold]</a>.

