  <tr><td><code>decltype(xs...&#91;I] + xs)... values</code></td><td>The second <code>xs</code></td><td>Expands the second <code>xs</code>. Indexing consumes only the first</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype((xs + ...))... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack of the fold's result type</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(sizeof...(xs))... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack of the <code>sizeof...</code> result type</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int... values[Is]</code></td><td><code>Is</code></td><td>Expands <code>Is</code> despite array parameter adjustment to <code>int*</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(requires { requires (C&lt;Ts&gt; &amp;&amp; ...); })... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous <code>bool</code> pack. Nested fold-expanded constraint consumes <code>Ts</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

## Pack declarations ## {#implicit-template}

The same declarator syntax covers ordinary expansions and homogeneous packs, whose patterns need not mention template parameters:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// heterogeneous function parameter pack
template <class... T>
void f(T... v);

// homogeneous function parameter pack
template <class T>
void f(T... v);

// homogeneous function parameter pack
void f(int... v);
</xmp>

R2 treated the pack length as an additional part of specialization identity even when the template had no formal parameters. [[P1219R2]] In this revision, a homogeneous function pack makes the declaration an abbreviated function template before body parsing, with an invented, unnamed constant template parameter representing its length. The length is therefore a template argument, whose value can be deduced or supplied explicitly where its position allows. For named packs, <code>sizeof...(values)</code> also exposes it.

The count has type <code>std::size_t</code>, matching <code>sizeof...</code>. To avoid requiring either a <code>std::size_t</code> declaration or a header, the wording refers to the type of a <code>sizeof</code> expression. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.sizeof#5">[expr.sizeof]/5</a>

There is a related concern in [[P1061R10]] section 3.3, which removed support for structured binding packs outside templates after objections to R9's implicit template region. That region covered the rest of an ordinary function after a structured binding declaration, whereas here the signature declares the whole function a template. This distinction does not, however, establish committee agreement on the proposal.

A homogeneous pack must be the only function parameter pack, appear last, and have no following C ellipsis. Non-pack parameters may precede it, as in [[#example-casadi]], leaving the remaining arguments to determine its count without pack or C-ellipsis partitioning rules. Once formed, its parameters behave like those of any function parameter pack.

For example, these declarations violate the placement rules:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void misplaced(int... values, int last);
void two_packs(int... left, double... right);
void trailing_varargs(int... values, ...);
</xmp>

The pack must also declare the function's or lambda call operator's own parameters. For example, <code>using Callback = void(int... values);</code> and <code>void register_callback(void (*callback)(int... values));</code> remain ill-formed because neither declares a function template to own the count. Supporting these forms would need separate count-introduction and deduction rules, while ordinary expansions in aliases and nested function types remain unchanged.

Although [[P2347R2]] proposes deducing the length of a single non-trailing function parameter pack from the number of arguments and required trailing parameters, non-trailing homogeneous packs remain outside this revision's scope.

### Abbreviated syntax ### {#abbreviated-syntax}

Since C++20, function templates needing no template parameter names can use abbreviated syntax with placeholders such as <code>auto</code> in the parameter list. [[P1141R2]] <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=249">[dcl.fct]/21</a>

Under our proposal, a fixed element type likewise leaves a template parameter for the count. When there are no explicit template parameters, the declaration omits the <code nohighlight>template-head</code>. An empty <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> still introduces an explicit specialization and cannot introduce a homogeneous pack.

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void named_f(int... values);
auto* named_pf = &named_f<3>;

template <class T>
void named_g(T... values);
auto* named_pg = &named_g<int, 3>;
</xmp>

Both pointers have type <code>void (*)(int, int, int)</code>, with the named packs and explicit counts requiring no comma-less reclamation. If reclamation is approved, it would also permit these unnamed equivalents:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void f(int...);
auto* pf = &f<3>;

template <class T>
void g(T...);
auto* pg = &g<int, 3>;
</xmp>

The count follows all explicit template parameters and invented <code>auto</code> type parameters without changing their positions:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class Tag>
void tagged(auto tag, int... values);
auto* tagged_pair = &tagged<void, char, 2>;
</xmp>

Here the arguments supply <code>Tag</code>, the invented <code>tag</code> type, and then the count, yielding pointer type <code>void (*)(char, int, int)</code>. The same order applies to lambda call operator templates.

The February 2019 Kona EWGI poll reproduced in R2 and <a href="#kona-ewgi-polls">Table 14</a> supports the named form but predates this revision and its count model. [[P1219R2]] [[P1219-EWG-RECORD]]

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Specify explicit-argument assignment when template parameter packs precede the count, including constant packs and <code>auto</code> parameters. Keep count last. Test call and target-type deduction where it cannot be explicit.</p>
</aside>

### Type deduction ### {#deduction}

To see how type deduction interacts with the count, consider these proposed functions. <code>sum</code> fixes the element type to <code>int</code>, whereas <code>total</code> uses the ordinary template parameter <code>T</code>.

<xmp class=language-cpp>
int sum(int... values) {
    return (0 + ... + values);
}

template <class T>
T total(T... values) {
    return (T{} + ... + values);
}
</xmp>

Without an explicit element type, <code>total</code> must deduce <code>T</code> consistently from every applicable argument, just as it would with separate <code>T</code> parameters. It neither takes the first argument's type nor computes a common type. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type#2">[temp.deduct.type]/2</a> For <code>sum</code> or <code>total&lt;int&gt;</code>, however, the element type is already known and ordinary conversions initialize the <code>int</code> parameters. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#6">[expr.call]/6</a> Supplying only <code>T</code> still leaves the count to be deduced, while supplying an explicit count fixes the length, as in <code>sum&lt;2&gt;(1, 2.5)</code> or <code>total&lt;int, 2&gt;(1, 2.5)</code>.

<figure class=table id=deduced-calls>
<figcaption id=deduced-calls-caption>Proposed calls. Counts are omitted and deduced from arity.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=deduced-calls-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Element type</th><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td rowspan=2>Fixed <code>int</code></td><td><code>sum()</code></td><td>Length zero. Initialized addition fold returns zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>sum(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Length two. Ordinary conversions initialize both parameters.</td></tr>
  <tr><td rowspan=3>Deduced <code>T</code></td><td><code>total(1, 2)</code></td><td>Deduces <code>T</code> as <code>int</code>, length two.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Reject conflicting deductions for <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total()</code></td><td>Reject: no argument to deduce <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td rowspan=2>Explicit <code>int</code></td><td><code>total&lt;int&gt;(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Length two, explicit type and ordinary conversions.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total&lt;int&gt;()</code></td><td>Length zero, known element type.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

The function template and its template arguments identify the specialization so different counts select different specializations. Conversions to a fixed element type do not add the original argument types to that identity. Therefore, <code>sum(1, 2)</code>, <code>sum(1.0, 2.0)</code>, and <code>sum&lt;2&gt;(1, 2)</code> all select <code>sum&lt;2&gt;</code>. This identity is the same whether the count was explicit or deduced, after conversion to the count parameter's type.

Equal function types alone do not, however, identify specializations, because different template arguments may produce the same adjusted function type. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.general#3">[temp.deduct.general]/3-4</a>

For <code>sum</code>, an explicit count must agree with the call's argument count. Under the proposal, <code>sum&lt;3&gt;(1, 2, 3)</code> and <code>sum&lt;0&gt;()</code> are therefore valid, while <code>sum&lt;3&gt;(1, 2)</code>, <code>sum&lt;2&gt;(1, 2, 3)</code>, and <code>sum&lt;0&gt;(1)</code> are rejected. Counts follow ordinary constant template argument rules for the invented unsigned type. An empty template-argument list supplies no count so <code>sum&lt;&gt;()</code> deduces zero, while <code>&amp;sum&lt;&gt;</code> needs a target type. Also, the invented parameter does not make every body expression dependent.

When the count is zero, the pack provides no arguments from which to deduce template parameters. The element type must therefore be explicit, deduced elsewhere, or defaulted. Even then, substitution into the element pattern still applies.

Ordinary rules also govern reference binding, parameter adjustment, and lifetimes. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> For example, braced arguments initialize their parameters with ordinary list-initialization and narrowing checks. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> Temporaries bound to reference parameters last through the call's full-expression. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/class.temporary#6.10">[class.temporary]/6.10</a> The pack does not extend that lifetime, nor does it change argument-initialization evaluation order. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#6">[expr.call]/6-7</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test deduction and initialization with move-only arguments, forwarding references, overloaded function names, braced arguments, constraints, exception specifications, and constant evaluation.</p>
</aside>

### Target types for initialization ### {#parameter-initialization}

A fixed parameter type also provides a target when an argument is a braced initializer or an overloaded function name. For example, consider these proposed declarations:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

void plot(const Point&... points);

using Step = void (*)();
void start();
template <class T> void start(T mode);
void stop();

void run_all(Step... steps);
</xmp>

<figure class=table id=target-typing>
<figcaption id=target-typing-caption>Target typing under the proposed parameter-initialization rules.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=target-typing-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>plot({0, 0}, {4, 5})</code></td><td>Each initializes a temporary <code>Point</code> bound to a const reference parameter.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>plot({2.5, 3})</code></td><td>Reject: list-initialization narrows <code>Point::x</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>run_all(start, stop)</code></td><td>The <code>Step</code> target selects <code>void start()</code>.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

With <code>auto...</code> parameters instead, deduction cannot infer types from bare braces or the <code>start</code> overload set containing a function template. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type#5">[temp.deduct.type]/5.8-5.9</a> Current C++ can still supply targets through <code>std::initializer_list&lt;Point&gt;</code> and <code>std::initializer_list&lt;Step&gt;</code> parameters, called as <code>plot({&#123;0, 0}, {4, 5}})</code> and <code>run_all({start, stop})</code>. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> Alternatively, callers can use explicit <code>Point</code> objects and <code>static_cast&lt;Step&gt;(start)</code> with deduced packs. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.over#1">[over.over]/1-3</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test braced initializers with constrained packs and initializer lists: deduction, empty calls and initializers, designated initializers, and narrowing.</p>
</aside>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Everyone: decision. Eczbek: input.</strong> Advise on deduction-guide deduction and return types before deciding support.</p>
</aside>

### Address-taking ### {#addresses}

The count can also be deduced when taking the address of <code>sum</code> or <code>total</code>. Here the target function type supplies the count, with a length-two <code>sum</code> specialization having the ordinary type <code>int(int, int)</code>.

<figure class=table id=address-taking-cases>
<figcaption id=address-taking-cases-caption>Proposed address-taking and declarations. Rows are independent. Element type and count must match the identified function.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=address-taking-cases-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Operation</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>int (*zero)() = sum</code></td><td>Select length zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*one)(int) = sum&lt;&gt;</code><br><code>int (*two)(int, int) = sum&lt;&gt;</code></td><td>Target types select different specializations.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;sum</code><br><code>auto two = &amp;sum&lt;&gt;</code></td><td>Reject: no length determined.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;total&lt;int&gt;</code></td><td>Reject: explicit <code>T</code> supplies no length.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;sum&lt;2&gt;</code><br><code>auto two = &amp;total&lt;int, 2&gt;</code></td><td>Select two-parameter specialization without a target type.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*two)(int, int) = sum&lt;3&gt;</code></td><td>Reject: explicit length mismatches target.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*two)(double, double) = sum</code></td><td>Reject: parameter-type mismatch. Call conversions cannot adapt function pointer signatures.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int sum(int...)</code> and the named definition</td><td>Same template, conditional on comma-less unnamed reclamation.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int sum(int, int)</code></td><td>Ordinary overload, not an implicit specialization declaration.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

The proposal also retains existing explicit-specialization and explicit-instantiation syntax. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.expl.spec#1">[temp.expl.spec]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.explicit#2">[temp.explicit]/2</a> For these declarations, omitted counts are deduced from the ordinary parameter lists:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <>
int sum<>(int value) {
    return value + 10;
}

template int sum<>(int, int);
</xmp>

Supplying the counts explicitly as <code>template &lt;&gt; int sum&lt;1&gt;(int value)</code> and <code>template int sum&lt;2&gt;(int, int)</code> identifies the same specialization and instantiation as above. The count must still match the parameter list. Whether the count is deduced or supplied through explicit substitution, ordinary target-type matching follows.

### Overload ordering ### {#overload-ordering}

The proposal retains ordinary conversion ranking, partial ordering, constraint subsumption, and element-type comparisons. Under these rules, unordered candidates remain ambiguous, without preference for homogeneous syntax. Partial ordering, in particular, compares declarations, preserving which parameters came from packs. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.best.general#2">[over.match.best.general]/2</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.partial#8">[temp.deduct.partial]/8</a> It therefore does not compare only resulting fixed-arity specializations. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.func.order#3">[temp.func.order]/3-6</a>

Calls without pack arguments contribute no element-type comparisons to partial ordering. Default template arguments can establish viability without making dependent patterns fixed types for ordering.

<figure class=table id=overload-ordering-cases>
<figcaption id=overload-ordering-cases-caption>Proposed overloads under ordinary ordering. Sets are independent.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=overload-ordering-cases-caption>
<colgroup><col><col><col></colgroup>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Overloads</th><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td rowspan=3><code>void fixed(int... values);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class T&gt; void fixed(T... values);</code></td>
<td><code>fixed(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select the fixed-element template.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fixed(1.0, 2.0)</code></td><td>Select the deduced-element template by better conversions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fixed()</code></td><td>Select fixed-element template: <code>T</code> cannot be deduced.</td></tr>
<tr><td rowspan=3><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void packed(T... values);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class... Ts&gt; void packed(Ts... values);</code></td>
<td><code>packed(1, 2)</code></td><td>Ambiguous.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>packed(1, 2.0)</code></td><td>Select heterogeneous template: homogeneous <code>T</code> deductions conflict.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>packed()</code></td><td>Select heterogeneous template: homogeneous <code>T</code> cannot be deduced.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void empty(int... values);</code><br>
<code>void empty(double... values);</code></td>
<td><code>empty()</code></td><td>Ambiguous: zero count supplies no element-type comparison.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void pair(int, int);</code><br>
<code>void pair(int... values);</code></td>
<td><code>pair(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select the non-template overload after tied conversions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void paired(T, T);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class T&gt; void paired(T... values);</code></td>
<td><code>paired(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select fixed-arity template by ordinary partial ordering.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void single(T);</code><br>
<code>void single(int... values);</code></td>
<td><code>single(1)</code></td><td>Ambiguous: neither the fixed-arity pattern nor fixed element type makes one template more specialized.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

For length-dependent associated constraints, count determination comes first. Satisfaction then substitutes ordinary template arguments, including the count and enclosing specializations' arguments, without separate count bindings. If the same atomic constraint is checked again with the same arguments, the existing consistency requirement still applies. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.atomic#3">[temp.constr.atomic]/3</a>

The count adds no atomic-identity or subsumption test. For example, separate predicates <code>sizeof...(values) &gt;= 1</code> and <code>sizeof...(values) &gt;= 2</code> gain no arithmetic implication rule. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.atomic#2">[temp.constr.atomic]/2</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.order#1.2">[temp.constr.order]/1.2</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test these overload outcomes with the invented count. Complete rules for matching corresponding packs in atomic and fold-expanded constraints.</p>
</aside>

### Constructors ### {#constructors}

If supported, homogeneous constructors would be implicit templates in ordinary overload selection. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.best.general#2.4">[over.match.best.general]/2.4</a> Initializer-list constructors, however, have priority under <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.list#1">[over.match.list]/1</a>.

<figure class=table id=constructor-comparison>
<figcaption id=constructor-comparison-caption>Conditional comparison of initializer-list and homogeneous constructors.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison" aria-labelledby=constructor-comparison-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>If homogeneous constructors are supported</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Values {
    Values(std::initializer_list<int>);
    explicit Values(const int&, const int&);
};
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Values {
    Values(const int&... elems);
    explicit Values(const int&, const int&);
};
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

<figure class=table id=constructor-selection>
<figcaption id=constructor-selection-caption>Constructor selection under ordinary ranking.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=constructor-selection-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Declaration</th><th scope=col>With initializer list</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous pack</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>Values values{1, 2, 3};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Selects the homogeneous constructor.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Values values{1, 2};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Selects the explicit two-argument constructor.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Values values = {1, 2};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Rejected: selected two-argument constructor is explicit.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Everyone: decision. Eczbek: input.</strong> Advise on constructor support and check these examples as implicit templates. Inclusion remains undecided.</p>
</aside>

## Lambdas ## {#lambdas}

The same count model also applies to lambdas. For example, this proposed lambda's call operator remains a template despite its fixed element type:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto product = [](double... factors) {
    return (1.0 * ... * factors);
};
</xmp>

Here, <code>product()</code> and <code>product(0.5, 0.8)</code> return <code>1.0</code> and <code>0.4</code>. The invented count also lets <code>product.operator()&lt;2&gt;(0.5, 0.8)</code> select the second specialization. A current-language <code>std::same_as&lt;double&gt; auto... factors</code> lambda accepts both ordinary calls, though its template arguments are element types rather than a count.

For <code>double (*multiply)(double, double) = product</code>, the captureless lambda's generic conversion function template deduces count two from the target. Unary <code>+product</code>, however, lacks a target type and count and is rejected. Ordinary restrictions on capture, explicit object parameters, constraints, and static call operators also remain. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.closure#12">[expr.prim.lambda.closure]/12-14</a>

If the lambda's parameters instead come from ordinary expansion of an enclosing pack, that expansion determines them even with indexing. It needs no independent count and alone does not make the lambda generic. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.general#6">[expr.prim.lambda.general]/6</a>

## Pack indexing ## {#indexing}

Homogeneous packs would support expansion, folding, and indexing using the same operations as existing packs. In particular, C++26 pack indexing provides direct access to an individual parameter by a constant index. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.pack.index#2">[expr.prim.pack.index]/2-4</a>

For example, count constraints and pack indexing would work alike for constrained and homogeneous packs:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t I>
double select_sample(double... samples)
    requires (I < sizeof...(samples)) {
    return samples...[I];
}
</xmp>

For example, with runtime <code>double reading</code>, <code>select_sample&lt;1&gt;(reading, 0.5, 1.0)</code> selects the second sample with a caller-supplied index and deduced length. Index 3 for these three samples fails the constraint. A <code>std::same_as&lt;double&gt; auto... samples</code> pack gives the same results. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.pack.index#2">[expr.prim.pack.index]/2-4</a>

Indexing can also select a type: <code>Ts...[1]</code> selects the second type of <code>Ts</code>. If the index is itself a pack, <code>Ts...[Is]</code> expands <code>Ts</code> alone, leaving the index pack <code>Is</code> for an enclosing expansion. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#6.15">[temp.variadic]/6.15</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.type.pack.index#2">[dcl.type.pack.index]/2-4</a>

In this valid C++26 example, each lambda has one unnamed parameter:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Ts, std::size_t... Is>
void indexed(std::index_sequence<Is...>) {
    (..., [](Ts...[Is]) {});
}
</xmp>

For example, <code>indexed&lt;int, double, char&gt;(std::index_sequence&lt;2, 0, 2, 1&gt;{})</code> forms four lambdas with parameter types <code>char</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>char</code>, and <code>double</code>. As this shows, the index pack can differ in length from <code>Ts</code> and repeat a type.

Rows in Table 12 are independent, with enclosing declarations of <code>Ts</code> and <code>Is</code> and valid selected indices.

<figure class=table id=indexed-lambda-parameters>
<figcaption id=indexed-lambda-parameters-caption>Indexed lambda parameters. A scalar index permits a new homogeneous count. An index pack expands in the enclosing declaration or fold.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=indexed-lambda-parameters-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Spelling</th><th scope=col>C++26 interpretation</th><th scope=col>Proposed interpretation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[1]) {}</code></td><td>One unnamed parameter of the selected type.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[1]) {})</code></td><td>Ill-formed. No unexpanded pack remains for the fold.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[Is]) {})</code></td><td>The fold expands <code>Is</code> into single-parameter lambdas.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[1]... values) {}</code></td><td>Ill-formed. No unexpanded pack.</td><td>A homogeneous pack of the selected type with its own count.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[Is]... values) {}</code></td><td>The second ellipsis expands <code>Is</code> into one parameter list.</td><td>Unchanged. No new count.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[Is]... values) {})</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The inner declaration has expanded <code>Is</code>, leaving none for the fold. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.fold#2">[expr.prim.fold]/2</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a></td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts... values) {}</code></td><td>Ordinary expansion of enclosing <code>Ts</code>, which alone does not make the lambda generic.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>


<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: advice. Eczbek: input.</strong> Resolve whether C++26 <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=250">[dcl.fct]/25</a> treats unnamed <code>Ts...[1]...</code> as C varargs. Check Clang parsing before settling the transition.</p>
</aside>

### Homogeneous packs of indexed types ### {#indexed-homogeneous}

For example, scalar indexing fixes the proposed pack's type before count deduction:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t I, class... Ts>
auto selected_counter() {
    return [](Ts...[I]... values) {
        return sizeof...(values);
    };
}

auto count_doubles = selected_counter<1, int, double, char>();
</xmp>

Here, <code>count_doubles(0.5, 1.0)</code> returns 2. The index selects <code>double</code> from three types and the call independently determines the count. Unlike <code>Ts...[Is]... values</code>, no index pack remains to expand.

### Array parameters ### {#arrays}

In C++26, <code>int... values[Is]</code> expands <code>Is</code> into array bounds, leaving none for an enclosing fold. For example, positive extents 1, 2, and 3 adjust to three <code>int*</code> parameters. Zero extents are independently invalid. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.array#1">[dcl.array]/1</a>

For unnamed packs, though, C++26 <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.name#1">[dcl.name]/1</a> has no array-suffix production for abstract (unnamed) pack declarators. P2662R3 removed it with pack indexing. [[P2662R3]]


The proposal retains this grammar. With approved reclamation, an array alias could supply an unnamed homogeneous parameter type with ordinary adjustment and an extent independent of its count. The parenthesized unnamed pack declarators tracked by <a href="https://cplusplus.github.io/CWG/issues/1488.html">CWG1488</a> remain out of scope because the alias needs no new declarator form.


### Empty packs ### {#empty-and-return}

An empty outer <code>Is</code> expansion need not instantiate indexed selection. In contrast, a fixed index must designate an element whenever its type is required, including in the proposed homogeneous lambda's declaration even for zero-argument calls. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#9">[temp.variadic]/9</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.type.pack.index#2">[dcl.type.pack.index]/2</a>

## Captures ## {#captures}

Constant template parameter packs need no capture, but the proposed function's parameters have finite lifetimes. For example, with runtime <code>int reading</code>, this callback stores copies: <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.capture">[expr.prim.lambda.capture]</a>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto deferred_sum(int... values) {
    return [=] { return (0 + ... + values); };
}

auto sum_later = deferred_sum(reading, 2, 3);
int reading_sum = sum_later();
</xmp>

For <code>reading == 1</code>, <code>reading_sum</code> is <code>6</code> after <code>deferred_sum</code> returns. Replacing <code>&#91;=]</code> with <code>[]</code> makes nonempty calls ill-formed. Reference capture leaves dangling references when <code>sum_later()</code> is called.

A current-language <code>std::same_as&lt;int&gt; auto... values</code> pack supports the same callback. Ordinary capture and lifetime rules also apply through nested lambdas. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.capture#14">[expr.prim.lambda.capture]/14</a>

## Alternatives ## {#alternatives}

We reject the alternative spellings, while deferring declaration-site lengths.

### An additional auto marker ### {#additional-auto}

If we consider <code>int auto... values</code>, the element type is already supplied. Here <code>auto</code> would mark template status rather than act as a placeholder, while <code>int</code> is not a concept constraint. We therefore reject this spelling. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.spec.auto.general">[dcl.spec.auto.general]</a>

Also, <code>std::size_t... values</code> already spells a homogeneous template parameter pack. Requiring <code>std::size_t auto... values</code> for function parameters would create a mismatch, while named <code>T... values</code> already supplies the type and pack unambiguously without another marker.

### Empty template heads ### {#empty-template-heads}

An empty template head could mark a template without naming its invented count:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK, declares a template with a homogeneous parameter pack
template <>
void foo(float... v);
</xmp>

This spelling is syntactically unambiguous here, but <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> already introduces an explicit specialization of an existing template. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.expl.spec#1">[temp.expl.spec]/1</a> The pack would therefore have to both establish template status and distinguish the prefix's roles. We retain the prefix for explicit specialization.

R2 also reports Richard Smith's opposition after San Diego in 2018. The subsequent 2019 Kona poll appears in [[#straw-polls]]. [[P1219R2]]

### Explicit template markers ### {#marker}

A marker could be required in the template parameter list:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <...>
void foo(int... v);

template <class T, ...>
void bar(T... v);
</xmp>

Such a marker would expose template status before the parameter list and could identify the count. It could also preserve optional names without reclaiming comma-less C varargs. For lambdas, the corresponding form would be <code>[]&lt;...&gt;(int... values) {}</code>, though genericity alone cannot change indexing or move <code>Is</code> between expansions.

For named packs, however, the parameters already establish template status, as they do in abbreviated declarations. [[P1141R2]] <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=249">[dcl.fct]/21</a> Requiring a marker would burden named declarations for a compatibility benefit confined to unnamed packs. We therefore reject required markers.

Restricting markers to unnamed packs would avoid that cost, but we prefer requiring a name to adding syntax solely for omitting it. This lets named packs proceed without reclamation ([[#compatibility]]). Also, R2's 2019 Clang experience did not establish a need for a marker, though it included no implementation experience from other compilers. [[P1219R2]]

### Bare template introducer ### {#bare-template-introducer}

R2 considered omitting brackets with no explicit template parameters: [[P1219R2]]

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template void foo(int... v);
</xmp>

This spelling is also unambiguous, but bare <code>template</code> already introduces explicit instantiation. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.explicit#1">[temp.explicit]/1-2</a> The homogeneous pack would distinguish the meanings here. Since abbreviated declarations can use that distinction without reusing the instantiation introducer, we reject the bare introducer.

### Explicit lengths ### {#explicit-lengths}

Declaration-site lengths are deferred. R2's pre-indexing spelling was: [[P1219R2]]

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t Len>
void foo(int...[Len] v);
</xmp>

Unlike an invented count, a declaration-site length would let the author name and position the count. This revision's trailing pack already supports count deduction, inspection with <code>sizeof...</code>, count constraints, and explicit count arguments where the position permits ([[#abbreviated-syntax]]). As these cover the present use cases, this revision needs no separately declared length.

There is also a conflict with the earlier spelling: <code>Ts...[Len]</code> already selects one type from <code>Ts</code> in C++26. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.type.pack.index">[dcl.type.pack.index]</a> A marker alone cannot resolve that conflict. This excludes the spelling, but does not rule out declaration-site lengths generally.

Named lengths could also accompany multiple or non-trailing homogeneous packs, though these extensions would need rules for associating and deducing their counts. We defer these extensions along with declaration-site lengths. Restoring the removed abstract array-pack grammar also remains a separate question.

# Reclaiming the deprecated comma-less spelling # {#compatibility}

We propose homogeneous packs and deprecated comma-less C-varargs removal for C++29, with separate polls for the two decisions ([[#proposed-polls]]). Reclamation would permit unnamed packs, but if EWG declines it, named packs can still proceed with the existing unnamed disambiguation.

Jan Schultke's [[P3176R1]], <em>The Oxford variadic comma</em>, proposed deprecation, not removal. Accordingly, N5050's C++26 deprecation preserves old meanings and standalone ellipsis parameters. [[N5050]] <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a> Under our proposed reclamation, <code>void send(int... values)</code> and <code>void send(int...)</code> would instead declare the same template, while C varargs would retain <code>void send(int, ...)</code>.

One standard cycle separates C++26 deprecation and proposed C++29 reclamation. Adding a comma preserves C varargs in earlier language modes, allowing migration before C++29. We cannot assess that migration from the 2019 open-source survey in [[P1219R2]], though. It predates deprecation and therefore establishes neither users' deprecation-warning experience nor remaining migration costs in maintained and vendored code.

There is a one-cycle precedent in [[P1161R3]]'s C++20 deprecation of unparenthesized comma subscripts and [[P2128R6]]'s proposed C++23 replacement. P2128R6 also deliberately withheld new multidimensional meaning from C arrays and existing <code>valarray</code> interfaces, however. It therefore cannot establish that immediate reinterpretation of comma-less varargs would be harmless. If one cycle is insufficient, EWG can choose a longer migration period.

## Current rules ## {#current-comma-rules}

A <em>varargs ellipsis</em> denotes C varargs rather than a parameter pack. Both C23 and C++26 permit standalone <code>...</code>. C++26 also permits omitting the comma between a parameter list and C varargs, but deprecates that spelling. [[N3096]] <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a>

A final ellipsis that could belong to an abstract (nameless) declarator declares a pack if its type names an unexpanded template parameter pack or contains <code>auto</code>. Otherwise it denotes C varargs. <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=250">[dcl.fct]/25</a>

These C++26 declarations therefore differ:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// declares a variadic function template with a function parameter pack
template <class... T>
void f(T...);

// same as void f(T, ...)
template <class T>
void f(T...);
</xmp>

## Proposed change ## {#proposed-comma-change}

Under reclamation, we would require a comma between a parameter list and C varargs and remove unnamed disambiguation, permitting unnamed homogeneous packs. If only named packs proceed, we would retain unnamed-ellipsis disambiguation instead. In either case, standalone <code>...</code>, comma-separated C varargs, and ordinary pack expansions would keep their meanings.

<figure class=table id=reclamation-compatibility>
<figcaption id=reclamation-compatibility-caption>Independent declarations before and after proposed C++29 reclamation. In the last row, <code>Ts</code> is an existing enclosing template type parameter pack.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=reclamation-compatibility-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>C++26 declaration</th><th scope=col>After reclamation</th><th scope=col>Migration preserving C varargs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>void f(int value...)</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The name precedes the ellipsis</td><td><code>void f(int value, ...)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void f(int...)</code></td><td>Homogeneous pack with fixed element type int</td><td><code>void f(int, ...)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template&lt;class T&gt; void f(T...)</code></td><td>Homogeneous pack with element type T</td><td><code>template&lt;class T&gt; void f(T, ...)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void f(Ts......)</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The final C ellipsis lacks a comma</td><td><code>void f(Ts..., ...)</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

For example, the six-dot declaration <code>template&lt;class... Ts&gt; struct S { static void f(Ts......); };</code> is valid today. A call to <code>S&lt;int&gt;::f(1, 2.0)</code> passes its second argument through C varargs. To preserve this interface, migration inserts a comma between the ellipses, including in named expansions: <code>Ts... values...</code> becomes <code>Ts... values, ...</code>. Removing the last ellipsis would instead change the interface. [[P3176R1]] <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a>

The first and last rows of Table 13 would become ill-formed and require diagnostics. The middle rows, however, would remain valid with new meanings and no diagnostic required merely for reclamation. For example, an old <code>f(1, 2.5)</code> call to <code>void f(int...)</code> passed a double through C varargs. Under reclamation, the same call would initialize two int parameters with an ordinary floating-integral conversion. Reclamation would also permit empty calls and yield fixed-count specializations without C ellipsis. Therefore, even successful recompilation would leave a source and potential link compatibility break under [[SD10]].

### Migrating definitions ### {#migrating-definitions}

Legacy definitions using <code>va_start</code> also need migration, because ordinary homogeneous parameters cannot preserve the actual C ellipsis they require. C++26 discards the second and subsequent <code>va_start</code> macro arguments so a surviving parameter name cannot portably trigger diagnostics. Although implementations may reject a builtin invocation in a non-variadic specialization, C-library preconditions do not require diagnostics. Retaining the C-varargs definition therefore requires inserting the comma. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/cstdarg.syn">[cstdarg.syn]</a>

If declarations and definitions are migrated inconsistently, they can validly declare a homogeneous template and a separate C-varargs overload. Calls may then become ambiguous or select the template. If that template has no definition, the omission can surface during instantiation or linking, though compile or link failure depends on the program, available definitions, and implementation. Changing a declaration provides no ABI compatibility with an existing C-varargs definition.

### Function types and overload resolution ### {#function-types-and-overload-resolution}

Function types also need migration. For example, <code>using F = void(int...)</code> would become ill-formed because function type aliases cannot introduce homogeneous function templates. It must become <code>using F = void(int, ...)</code> to retain C varargs. Reclaimed declarations would no longer match C-varargs pointer targets, though fixed-arity targets could select a specialization by deducing its count ([[#addresses]]).

Reclamation would also replace ellipsis conversion sequences with ordinary parameter conversions, potentially changing overload resolution and answers from well-formed metaprogramming that probes callability, takes addresses, or recognizes C-varargs function types. To preserve C-varargs intent, commas must therefore be retained across declarations, definitions, pointer and member-pointer types, traits, and macro-generated declarations. An ordinary expansion such as <code>template&lt;class... Ts&gt; void f(Ts...)</code> would remain unchanged.

Reclamation also needs to be coordinated with [[P3776R1]], <em>More trailing commas</em>, which proposes trailing parameter-list commas except after C ellipsis parameters. That grammar coordination includes the comma between ordinary packs and C varargs.

# Straw polls # {#straw-polls}

## Proposed EWG polls ## {#proposed-polls}

These proposed direction polls for P1219R3 have not been taken.

1. We want named homogeneous function parameter packs for C++29: one trailing pack, ordinary parameter initialization, and an invented constant template parameter for its count, independently of unnamed-syntax reclamation.
2. We want to remove deprecated comma-less C varargs in C++29 and reclaim the spelling for unnamed homogeneous function parameter packs.

Poll 1 can proceed independently, while poll 2 would make parameter names optional. If EWG requests it, removal can be scheduled in a separate paper without changing named packs.

