## Variadic templates ## {#wording-variadic}

In <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#2">[temp.variadic]/2</a>, insert after the first sentence:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and addition</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>A function parameter pack is a function parameter that accepts zero or more function arguments.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>For a <var class=grammar ignore>parameter-declaration</var> that declares a function parameter pack, its <em>parameter pattern</em> is the <var class=grammar ignore>parameter-declaration</var> with the ellipsis in its declarator or <var class=grammar ignore>abstract-declarator</var> removed. The unexpanded packs in the parameter pattern are determined after introducing any template parameter corresponding to a placeholder type in the declaration.</ins></p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>If the parameter pattern contains no unexpanded packs, the declaration declares a <em>homogeneous function parameter pack</em>. Otherwise, it declares a function parameter pack that is a pack expansion whose pattern is the parameter pattern. This classification is determined before substituting template arguments from an enclosing templated entity and is not changed by that substitution.</ins></p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>A homogeneous function parameter pack shall declare parameters of the function declared by a function declaration or of a lambda's function call operator template.</ins></p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>A homogeneous function parameter pack has an associated invented constant template parameter, its <em>count parameter</em>. The type of the count parameter is the type of a <code>sizeof</code> expression (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.sizeof">[expr.sizeof]</a>). The argument for the count parameter is the <em>pack count</em> and specifies the number of function parameters introduced by the pack. A homogeneous function parameter pack shall be the last parameter of its <var class=grammar ignore>parameter-declaration-clause</var>. That <var class=grammar ignore>parameter-declaration-clause</var> shall have the form <var class=grammar ignore>parameter-declaration-list</var>. No other parameter of that clause shall be a function parameter pack.</ins></p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>The type of each parameter introduced by a homogeneous function parameter pack is obtained from its parameter pattern by substitution and the parameter type adjustments specified in <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a>. Substitution into the element type is performed even when the pack count is zero. The parameters so introduced are function parameters for all other purposes. The value of a <code>sizeof...</code> expression naming this pack is the argument for its count parameter.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

Replace <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#6.1">[temp.variadic]/6.1</a> with:

<blockquote class=wording-inline>
<p>In a function parameter pack <ins>that is a pack expansion </ins>(<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a>)<del>;</del><ins>,</ins> the pattern is <del>the <var class=grammar ignore>parameter-declaration</var> without the ellipsis</del><ins>its parameter pattern</ins>.</p>
</blockquote>

Replace the first three sentences of <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a> with:

<blockquote class=wording-inline>
<p><del>A pack whose</del><ins>An occurrence of a</ins> name <del>appears</del><ins>denoting a pack is unexpanded in a construct unless it is</ins> within the pattern of a pack expansion <del>is expanded by that pack expansion</del><ins>contained in that construct</ins>.<ins> A pack is unexpanded in a construct if the construct contains at least one such occurrence. An occurrence that declares a pack is not considered when determining unexpanded packs.</ins></p>
