## Dependency ## {#wording-dependency}

Under our proposal, a homogeneous-pack function type depends on its invented count until that count is determined, even when its element type is non-dependent. Until then, <code>sizeof...</code> naming the pack is also value-dependent. Count substitution still follows ordinary template rules and declaring the pack does not make unrelated expressions dependent.

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Check dependency with a fixed element type and unknown count, and substitution failure in pack expressions, indexing, and count constraints, including an invalid indexed element type at count zero.</p>
</aside>

## Deduction ## {#wording-deduction}

In <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.general#1">[temp.deduct.general]/1</a>, insert before Example 1:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and addition</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>When a function template specialization is referenced, all of the template arguments shall have values. The values can be explicitly specified or, in some cases, be deduced from the use or obtained from default <var class=grammar ignore>template-argument</var>s.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>For a function template that declares a homogeneous function parameter pack, the argument for its count parameter shall be determined. The argument can be explicitly specified or deduced as described in <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call">[temp.deduct.call]</a> and <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type">[temp.deduct.type]</a>. After substitution, the pack introduces the number of function parameters specified by that argument, each formed from its parameter pattern. Explicit specification and deduction of equivalent count arguments identify the same specialization under the ordinary template-argument equivalence rules.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

