### Function calls ### {#wording-call-deduction}

In <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a>, make the inline change and insert before Example 2:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and changes</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>For a function parameter pack that occurs at the end of the <var class=grammar ignore>parameter-declaration-list</var>, deduction is performed for each remaining argument of the call, taking the type <em>P</em> of the <var class=grammar ignore>declarator-id</var> of the function parameter pack as the corresponding function template parameter type. Each deduction deduces template arguments for subsequent positions in the template parameter packs<ins> (if any)</ins> expanded by the function parameter pack. When a function parameter pack appears in a non-deduced context (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type">[temp.deduct.type]</a>), the type of that pack is never deduced.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>For a trailing homogeneous function parameter pack whose count argument has not been explicitly specified, deduce that argument as the number of call arguments remaining after those associated with the preceding parameters. If the count argument has been explicitly specified, the number of remaining call arguments shall equal it. Deduction from the parameter pattern is performed independently for each corresponding argument for template parameters whose arguments have not been explicitly specified. Deductions for the same template parameter, including those from preceding function parameters, are combined under the ordinary deduction rules. If no call arguments remain, deduce an omitted count as zero and make no element-type deductions from the pack.</ins></p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>After deduction and substitution, each argument initializes its corresponding function parameter under the ordinary initialization rules. An explicitly supplied element type is not deduced again. An argument-count mismatch or inconsistent deduction causes template argument deduction to fail.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test deduction with preceding default arguments, explicit object parameters, pack-expansion arguments, empty packs, and conversions to fixed or explicitly supplied element types.</p>
</aside>

Retain the existing <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call">[temp.deduct.call]</a> example unchanged.

### Function types ### {#wording-type-deduction}

In <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type#10">[temp.deduct.type]/10</a>, make the inline change and insert immediately after the sentence shown below:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and changes</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>Each comparison deduces template arguments for subsequent positions in the template parameter packs<ins> (if any)</ins> expanded by the function parameter pack.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>When a function parameter list containing a trailing homogeneous function parameter pack is matched against a function parameter list of known length, deduce an omitted argument for the count parameter as the number of parameters remaining after matching the preceding parameters. If an explicitly supplied count differs from that number, template argument deduction fails. Match the parameter pattern against each remaining parameter using the applicable deduction rules. If no parameters remain, deduce an omitted count as zero and make no element-type deductions from the pack. This deduction applies when identifying a specialization from a target function type or from an explicit specialization or explicit instantiation declaration. It does not apply call-argument conversions. In the absence of an explicit count and a function type from which to deduce it, the count remains undetermined and no specialization is identified.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test matching against target function types, explicit specializations, and instantiations, including parameter type adjustments. Reject undetermined counts and type mismatches requiring call-site conversions.</p>
</aside>

### Partial ordering and constraints ### {#wording-partial-ordering}

This candidate wording specifies [[#overload-ordering]].

Insert after <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.func.order#3">[temp.func.order]/3</a>:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and addition</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>To produce the transformed template, for each type, constant, type template, variable template, or concept template parameter (including template parameter packs (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic">[temp.variadic]</a>) thereof) synthesize a unique type, value, class template, variable template, or concept, respectively, and substitute it for each occurrence of that parameter in the function type of the template. The type replacing a placeholder in the type of the value synthesized for a constant template parameter is a unique synthesized type.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>The transformation preserves each homogeneous function parameter pack and applies the usual transformation to all template parameters, including its count parameter. The parameter pattern is not expanded to the count deduced for a candidate specialization.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

In <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.partial#8">[temp.deduct.partial]/8</a>, make the inline change and insert immediately after the sentence shown below:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and changes</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>Each comparison deduces template arguments for subsequent positions in the template parameter packs<ins> (if any)</ins> expanded by the function parameter pack.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>For partial ordering, the pattern of a homogeneous function parameter pack is its parameter pattern (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic">[temp.variadic]</a>). Deduction against this pattern combines deductions for template parameters under the usual consistency requirements. Comparison of two pack patterns does not determine their count arguments from the arity of a candidate specialization. A parameter transformed from a homogeneous function parameter pack remains a parameter transformed from a function parameter pack for the purposes of this subclause.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

Existing tie-breaks apply without a new preference for homogeneous packs.

## Declarations ## {#wording-correspondence}

Corresponding homogeneous packs have corresponding parameter patterns and invented count parameters in the same template parameter position. Parameter names do not distinguish declarations. An ordinary pack expansion does not correspond to a homogeneous pack solely because a specialization produces the same function type.

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test declaration and specialization identity across translation units, modules, and serialized ASTs, checking definition visibility and explicit instantiations separately from ABI representation.</p>
</aside>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: advice. Eczbek: input.</strong> Confirm PoC reflection support. If supported, also test count exposure in template-argument order, function-template identification, and resulting parameters, including none at count zero.</p>
</aside>

## Feature-test macro ## {#wording-feature-test}

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Everyone: decision. Eczbek: input.</strong> Agree the implementation's feature-test macro and revision value with SG10 once feature scope is settled.</p>
</aside>

# Conclusion # {#conclusion}

We seek agreement on one trailing homogeneous function parameter pack for C++29 with ordinary parameter initialization and an invented constant template parameter for length. The count argument can be explicit or deduced from calls or target function types. In either case, the specializations have ordinary fixed-arity function types. We leave declaration-site length syntax and multiple homogeneous packs out of scope.

Within this scope, the named feature, including explicit counts, can proceed independently of our separate C++29 request to remove deprecated comma-less C varargs and reclaim the spelling for unnamed homogeneous packs. If EWG feedback warrants it, removal may move to a separate paper. Reclamation, however, changes existing meanings and the usage evidence is limited. EWG must therefore still decide whether one standard cycle suffices for migration.

